{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"c2575c6b414c8162c7cfbcbc5553c5bc197e271f"},"timestamp":1571592841,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU1MDk2MjU2MzM=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3816","origin":"github"},"title":"Error with latest git-annex version and initremote --sameas","message":"In my dataset,I set up two special rsync remotes using `git-annex initremote NAS type=rsync ...` and `git-annex initremote NASuser --sameas NAS type=rsync .. autoenable=true.`, pointing to the same data source, but having different file permissions (`NASuser`is read-only).\n\nNow, when I publish the dataset and install it somewhere else, I receive the error\n```\n[INFO   ]   Remote origin not usable by git-annex; setting annex-ignore \n[ERROR  ] sequence item 0: expected str instance, NoneType found [utils.py:_handle_possible_annex_dataset:192] (TypeError) \n```\nAlso, the special remote `NASuser`is not enabled automatically, and indeed, enabling it via `datalad siblings enable -s NASuser` is not successful, giving\n```\n[ERROR  ] unknown sibling name [query-sibling(/home/eckhard/Documents/Research/DataLad/testdemo)] \n```\nUsing `git-annex enableremote NASuser` instead seems to work, resulting in\n```\nenableremote NASuser ok\n(recording state in git...)\n```\nSo, while the problem can be easily ignored for individual datasets, it does prevent me from doing recursive installs.\n\nIs the `--sameas` option of `git-annex initremote` known to be problematic and are there plans to make `datalad`compatible with it?\nThanks!","files":null}]}